      "text": "To find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 1407 and 903, we can use the Euclidean algorithm, which is an efficient method for computing the greatest common divisor of two numbers. The steps are as follows:\n\n1. **Divide 1407 by 903 and find the remainder:**\n   \\[\n   1407 \\div 903 = 1 \\quad \\text{with a remainder of} \\quad 1407 - 903 \\times 1 = 504\n   \\]\n   So, \\(1407 = 903 \\times 1 + 504\\).\n\n2. **Now, apply the Euclidean algorithm to 903 and 504:**\n   \\[\n   903 \\div 504 = 1 \\quad \\text{with a remainder of} \\quad 903 - 504 \\times 1 = 399\n   \\]\n   So, \\(903 = 504 \\times 1 + 399\\).\n\n3. **Next, apply the Euclidean algorithm to 504 and 399:**\n   \\[\n   504 \\div 399 = 1 \\quad \\text{with a remainder of} \\quad 504 - 399 \\times 1 = 105\n   \\]\n   So, \\(504 = 399 \\times 1 + 105\\).\n\n4. **Now, apply the Euclidean algorithm to 399 and 105:**\n   \\[\n   399 \\div 105 = 3 \\quad \\text{with a remainder of} \\quad 399 - 105 \\times 3 = 48\n   \\]\n   So, \\(399 = 105 \\times 3 + 48\\).\n\n5. **Next, apply the Euclidean algorithm to 105 and 48:**\n   \\[\n   105 \\div 48 = 2 \\quad \\text{with a remainder of} \\quad 105 - 48 \\times 2 = 19\n   \\]\n   So, \\(105 = 48 \\times 2 + 19\\).\n\n6. **Now, apply the Euclidean algorithm to 48 and 19:**\n   \\[\n   48 \\div 19 = 2 \\quad \\text{with a remainder of} \\quad 48 - 19 \\times 2 = 8\n   \\]\n   So, \\(48 = 19 \\times 2 + 8\\).\n\n7. **Next, apply the Euclidean algorithm to 19 and 8:**\n   \\[\n   19 \\div 8 = 2 \\quad \\text{with a remainder of} \\quad 19 - 8 \\times 2 = 3\n   \\]\n   So, \\(19 = 8 \\times 2 + 3\\).\n\n8. **Now, apply the Euclidean algorithm to 8 and 3:**\n   \\[\n   8 \\div 3 = 2 \\quad \\text{with a remainder of} \\quad 8 - 3 \\times 2 = 2\n   \\]\n   So, \\(8 = 3 \\times 2 + 2\\).\n\n9. **Next, apply the Euclidean algorithm to 3 and 2:**\n   \\[\n   3 \\div 2 = 1 \\quad \\text{with a remainder of} \\quad 3 - 2 \\times 1 = 1\n   \\]\n   So, \\(3 = 2 \\times 1 + 1\\).\n\n10. **Finally, apply the Euclidean algorithm to 2 and 1:**\n    \\[\n    2 \\div 1 = 2 \\quad \\text{with a remainder of} \\quad 2 - 1 \\times 2 = 0\n    \\]\n    So, \\(2 = 1 \\times 2 + 0\\).\n\nSince the remainder is now 0, the GCD is the last non-zero remainder, which is 1.\n\nTherefore, the greatest common divisor of 1407 and 903 is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).",
