      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of two numbers, we can use the Euclidean algorithm. This algorithm is based on the principle that the GCD of two numbers also divides their difference. The steps of the algorithm are as follows:\n\nLet's find the GCD of 1407 and 903.\n\n1. Divide 1407 by 903 and find the remainder.\n   \\[\n   1407 = 903 \\cdot 1 + 504\n   \\]\n   So, the remainder is 504.\n\n2. Now, apply the Euclidean algorithm to 903 and 504.\n   \\[\n   903 = 504 \\cdot 1 + 399\n   \\]\n   So, the remainder is 399.\n\n3. Next, apply the Euclidean algorithm to 504 and 399.\n   \\[\n   504 = 399 \\cdot 1 + 105\n   \\]\n   So, the remainder is 105.\n\n4. Now, apply the Euclidean algorithm to 399 and 105.\n   \\[\n   399 = 105 \\cdot 3 + 84\n   \\]\n   So, the remainder is 84.\n\n5. Next, apply the Euclidean algorithm to 105 and 84.\n   \\[\n   105 = 84 \\cdot 1 + 21\n   \\]\n   So, the remainder is 21.\n\n6. Now, apply the Euclidean algorithm to 84 and 21.\n   \\[\n   84 = 21 \\cdot 4 + 0\n   \\]\n   So, the remainder is 0.\n\nSince the remainder is now 0, the GCD is the last non-zero remainder, which is 21.\n\nTherefore, the greatest common divisor of 1407 and 903 is \\boxed{21}.",
